# Regular Expressions (Regex)

Regular expressions have two common forms: **basic** and **extended**.

Most commands that use regular expressions can interpret **basic regular expressions**.  
However, **extended regular expressions** are not available for all commands and usually require a command option to work correctly (example: `egrep` or `grep -E`).

---

## Basic Regular Expression Characters

| Basic Regex Character(s) | Meaning |
|--------------------------|---------|
| `.` | any one single character |
| `[ ]` | any one specified character |
| `[^ ]` | not the one specified character |
| `*` | zero or more of the previous character |
| `^` | if first character in the pattern, match must be at the beginning of the line (otherwise literal `^`) |
| `$` | if last character in the pattern, match must be at the end of the line (otherwise literal `$`) |

---

## Extended Regular Expression Characters

Extended regular expressions must be used with either the `egrep` command or the `-E` option with the `grep` command.

| Extended Regex Character(s) | Meaning |
|----------------------------|---------|
| `+` | one or more of the previous pattern |
| `?` | the preceding pattern is optional |
| `{ }` | specify minimum, maximum, or exact matches of the previous pattern |
| `|` | alternation (logical OR) |
| `( )` | used to create groups |
